# Production environment — copy, fill in, and keep the real one out of git.
#
#   cp .env.prod.example .env.prod
#   docker compose --env-file .env.prod -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d
#
# Better still, do not have a real file at all: inject these from a secret
# manager. A file on the host is a file that gets copied, backed up and left in
# a home directory.
#
# `docker-compose.prod.yml` marks every one of these as required, so a missing
# value fails at `up` with a message rather than starting a misconfigured
# stack. That is the point of the file — see its header.
#
# TWO THINGS THAT CHANGED IN PHASE 38
#
# 1. This file is now delivered **whole** into the backend and persona
#    containers, through `env_file: ${APP_ENV_FILE:-.env}`. Before that, a
#    service saw only the names enumerated by hand in the YAML and everything
#    else was inert. So anything in `.env.example` can be set here and it will
#    arrive — and anything not set takes the default compiled into
#    `backend/src/config.ts`, which is where every default lives.
#
# 2. The cost of delivering it whole: the application container's environment
#    now contains every secret in this file, including the database superuser
#    password and the Grafana admin password, which the application itself does
#    not use. If that matters for your deployment, keep those two out of this
#    file and inject them only where they are needed — compose reads them for
#    `${VAR}` substitution from `--env-file`, which is a separate mechanism from
#    what is handed to a container.
#
# Only the variables that differ from `.env.example` are listed. Everything
# else keeps its documented default; read `.env.example` for what those are and
# why.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHICH FILE THIS IS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compose reads this file twice, for two different jobs, and they are not the
# same job. `--env-file .env.prod` populates ${VAR} substitutions inside the YAML;
# `env_file:` inside a service is what actually puts variables into a container.
# The second one needs a path, and a fixed path would have served development
# values to every profile — so each file names itself here and the service
# reads `${APP_ENV_FILE:-.env}`.
#
# Self-referential on purpose: the value arrives by the same mechanism it
# configures, so a profile that forgets this line falls back to `.env` rather
# than to nothing at all.
APP_ENV_FILE=.env.prod

# --- The public identity ----------------------------------------------------

# ALLOWED_ORIGINS and TRUST_PROXY_HOPS are set once each, in the TLS section
# below, beside the domain and the proxy chain they depend on. They were also
# assigned here, and the two copies of TRUST_PROXY_HOPS disagreed: this one
# said 1, the one below says 2, and the last assignment is the one that runs.
# 2 is correct for this stack - Caddy in front of nginx is two hops - so the
# duplicate was documenting a value the stack never used.

# Where the TLS terminator should proxy to. Loopback only.
FRONTEND_BIND_PORT=8080

# --- Secrets ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Salts every IP hash. Long and random — `openssl rand -hex 32`. Changing it
# later invalidates every existing IP-dimension block, so choose once.
IP_HASH_SALT=

# The application's connection string. This role holds DML on its own tables
# and nothing else: no CREATE, no access to other databases (§8.1).
DATABASE_URL=postgres://karobakbak_app:CHANGE_ME@postgres:5432/karobakbak

# The migration role, which is *not* the application's. It may CREATE; the
# application may not, and could not run migrations even if asked to.
MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgres://karobakbak:CHANGE_ME@postgres:5432/karobakbak

# The PostgreSQL container's own superuser, if the database runs here rather
# than as a managed service.
POSTGRES_USER=karobakbak
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_DB=karobakbak

GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# LOOPBACK, AND THIS IS NOT A PLACEHOLDER TO REPLACE WITH THE REAL DOMAIN.
#
# Grafana is bound to 127.0.0.1 in `docker-compose.prod.yml` and Caddy has
# exactly one route (`reverse_proxy frontend:80`). Nothing serves /grafana and
# nothing is meant to: an admin login published to the internet is the thing
# that binding avoids. The supported way in is a tunnel from your own machine —
#
#   ssh -L 3000:localhost:3000 deploy@<DROPLET_IP>
#
# — so `http://localhost:3000` is the origin the browser really sees, the same
# rule AUTH_REDIRECT_URI follows above.
#
# This said `https://karobakbak.com/grafana` for the first deployment, which
# broke the only access route there is. A sub-path in root_url without
# GF_SERVER_SERVE_FROM_SUB_PATH=true makes Grafana serve at / while building
# every redirect under /grafana: / -> /grafana -> / until the browser gives up
# with ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS. It fails identically at every origin, so it is
# not a tunnel problem, and the error names cookies, which is a false lead.
#
# Publishing it later means a Caddy route, a sub-path Grafana agrees to serve,
# and a decision about exposing an admin login — three things, not this line.
GRAFANA_ROOT_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- How this reaches Redis (D13) -------------------------------------------
#
# Redis holds every piece of live matchmaking state, and /ready is derived from
# its health alone -- so losing it takes every backend out of the load balancer
# at once. Leave this section as it is and that is the deployment you have: one
# node, and its failure is total. That is legal, documented, and warned about at
# startup.
#
# The two shapes below are mutually exclusive and setting both is a startup
# failure. Nothing here ever falls back to a single node quietly: a topology
# that looked configured and was not would only reveal itself on the night it
# was bought to survive.

# One node at a fixed address. The compose default reaches the container.
# REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379

# Or a Sentinel set, which is what survives losing the node. Blank REDIS_URL
# above -- a bare `REDIS_URL=` -- when you set these.
#
# Three minimum. Two is the worst possible number: losing either leaves the
# other short of a majority, so nothing is promoted and the topology is worse
# than the single node it replaced.
#
# The replica has to be on a **second machine** to be worth anything. All of
# this on one host protects against a Redis process crashing, and the failure
# that actually takes the service down is the host.
#
# Drill it before trusting it: docs/OPERATIONS.md §17.
# REDIS_SENTINELS=10.0.0.11:26379,10.0.0.12:26379,10.0.0.13:26379
# REDIS_SENTINEL_NAME=karobakbak

# Only if the nodes require AUTH. The sentinels and the data nodes are separate
# processes and routinely hold different credentials; passing one where the
# other belongs connects successfully and then fails every command.
#
# Redis here has no password and no TLS, which is defensible only while it is
# reachable solely from inside the compose network. Replication across hosts is
# not that -- set these and put the link on a private network.
# REDIS_PASSWORD=
# REDIS_SENTINEL_PASSWORD=

# --- Operational ------------------------------------------------------------

LOG_LEVEL=info
PROMETHEUS_RETENTION=30d

# --- Advertising ------------------------------------------------------------

# The provider stays a no-op until one is actually chosen (ads §21).
# Required. Where encrypted dumps are copied, as an rclone remote; configure the
# remote's credentials in ops/backup/rclone.conf. Backups that never leave the
# database host are lost by the failure most likely to need them.
#
# Cloudflare R2: 10 GB free, no egress charge, and one fewer vendor to hold
# credentials for. It is outside India, so it is a disclosed cross-border
# transfer — see the Privacy Policy's "Where your data is", which names the
# provider. Changing this destination, or adding a second one, changes that
# section too.
BACKUP_REMOTE=r2:<backup-bucket>

# Required. The age gate links both, and they must be reachable without passing
# it. The production compose refuses to build if either is unset.
VITE_TERMS_URL=https://karobakbak.com/terms
VITE_PRIVACY_URL=https://karobakbak.com/privacy

# Where the footer's "Buy me a coffee" points while payments are off.
#
# A link and never an embed: a donation widget is a third-party script, and
# the Privacy Policy states the site makes no third-party requests from the
# browser. An anchor requests nothing until somebody clicks it and leaves.
# Empty hides the link rather than pointing it nowhere.
VITE_SUPPORT_URL=

VITE_ADS_ENABLED=false
VITE_ADS_PROVIDER=noop

# The shared secret the backup job presents when it reports a run.
#
# Empty means the report route does not exist at all — the correct posture for a
# deployment that is not taking backups, and also the reason `BackupMonitor`
# would stay at `unknown` for ever, which `BackupsNeverObserved` alerts on once
# the process has been up longer than BACKUP_MAX_AGE_SECONDS.
#
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# The backup container reads the same value from ops/backup/report-token, and
# nothing checks that the two agree. When they disagree the backend answers 401,
# backup.sh logs "could not reach ... to report", and backups keep succeeding
# while the monitor never sees one.
#
# Write that file WITHOUT a trailing newline and never with CRLF:
#   printf %s "$TOKEN" > ops/backup/report-token
# A carriage return there malforms the Authorization header, which produces a
# 400 and the same misleading "could not reach" line. Found by drill, 2026-09-04;
# backup.sh now refuses to start on it rather than reporting into the void.
BACKUP_REPORT_TOKEN=

# How often the backup job runs, and how many encrypted dumps it keeps.
BACKUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=86400
BACKUP_KEEP=7

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TLS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The public hostname. Caddy obtains and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate for
# exactly this name, so DNS for it must already point at this host before the
# stack starts — the ACME challenge arrives on port 80 and fails otherwise.
#
# The registered domain is `karobakbak.com`. It is the apex, deliberately:
# `www.karobakbak.com` is a **different origin**, so serving both means either a
# second entry in ALLOWED_ORIGINS or — better — a redirect to one canonical
# name. Two live origins would also mean the block-token cookie is set on
# whichever one the visitor happened to use, and a person who arrives on the
# other one silently loses their blocks.
KAROBAKBAK_DOMAIN=karobakbak.com

# Where Let's Encrypt sends warnings if renewal ever stops working. A real
# address you read; this is the only notice you get before a certificate
# expires.
ACME_EMAIL=

# How many proxies sit in front of the backend. Caddy plus nginx is 2.
# A CDN in front makes it 3, and Cloudflare must then also be added to
# `trusted_proxies` in ops/caddy/Caddyfile.
#
# Too low and every user resolves to nginx's address: one rate-limit bucket and
# one block identity for the entire service, so a ban on anyone bans everyone.
# Too high and the backend reads an entry the client wrote, so the client
# address becomes something a sender can choose, which undermines per-IP limits
# and blocks.
#
# 2 is the count for Caddy -> nginx -> backend. Cloudflare in front
# makes it 3 **and** requires Cloudflare in `trusted_proxies` in the Caddyfile —
# the same commit, because trusting it there is what stops Caddy discarding the
# real client address, and it also stops Caddy discarding a forged one.
#
# Measure it against the running stack rather than taking either number on
# trust: OPERATIONS.md §7d, "Measuring TRUST_PROXY_HOPS".
TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=2

# The public origin, exactly as a browser sends it: scheme and host, no path,
# no trailing slash. An empty allowlist is a startup failure in production.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://karobakbak.com

# How long an untriaged report may wait before `ReportsWaitingTooLong` pages.
#
# Exported as a metric so the alert compares against this value rather than
# repeating it — a threshold copied into a rule file drifts the moment somebody
# tunes one and not the other.
#
# Set it to something you will actually meet. A target missed every day is the
# same as no target, except that it also trains you to ignore the alert.
REPORT_RESPONSE_TARGET_SECONDS=86400
REPORT_QUEUE_SAMPLE_MS=60000

# The address the Contact page and footer show.
VITE_CONTACT_EMAIL=contact@karobakbak.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SIGN-IN (OIDC / Cognito, §32, D48)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS BLOCK WAS MISSING, AND ITS ABSENCE IS NOT NEUTRAL.
#
# `ACCOUNTS_ENABLED` in `frontend/src/features.ts` is a compiled-in constant and
# is **true**, so every production bundle renders a sign-in button. The
# backend's `AUTH_ENABLED` defaults to false, and this file said nothing about
# it — so the first real deployment served that button into four routes that
# were never registered. `curl https://<domain>/auth/start` answered 404 and the
# UI failed the way `features.ts` predicts in its own comment: "the header
# offers a button into a 404".
#
# So there are exactly two supported states, and no third:
#
#   1. Fill this block in. Sign-in works.
#   2. Set ACCOUNTS_ENABLED=false in features.ts and rebuild the frontend.
#      That is a commit, not a setting — see the note on those constants.
#
# Leaving AUTH_ENABLED=false with the shipped frontend is state three, and it
# is the one that looks broken to every visitor.
#
# Signing in is FREE. It gates saved connections, recent history and friend
# requests, and is never coupled to PAYMENTS_ENABLED (§31).
#
# A half-filled block is refused at startup by name rather than degraded, so a
# missing value is a container that will not start, not a button that fails.
AUTH_ENABLED=true

# The pool. Checked against the `iss` claim, so it must match exactly, and must
# be https. See docs/operations/SIGNIN-SETUP.md for the live values.
AUTH_ISSUER=

# The OAuth endpoints, which on Cognito live on the *hosted UI domain* — a
# different host from the issuer above. Using the issuer host here is the
# common first mistake.
AUTH_AUTHORIZE_URL=https://auth.karobakbak.com/oauth2/authorize
AUTH_TOKEN_URL=https://auth.karobakbak.com/oauth2/token

# Where the signing keys are published: the issuer host, not the login UI.
AUTH_JWKS_URL=

# The app client. The secret is required for a confidential client. Never commit
# a real value — this file is tracked; `.env.prod` is not.
AUTH_CLIENT_ID=
AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# THE ORIGIN THE BROWSER SEES. The backend publishes no host port; /auth/* is
# proxied through the frontend container and Caddy, so this is the public
# origin. It must be registered on the Cognito app client byte-for-byte.
AUTH_REDIRECT_URI=https://karobakbak.com/auth/callback

# Skip Cognito's own form and go straight to the federated provider. Must exist
# on the pool AND be ticked on the app client — the second step is the one that
# gets missed. `SIGNIN_PROVIDER` in features.ts is 'google', so this should say
# Google or the button's wording will not match where it lands.
AUTH_IDENTITY_PROVIDER=Google

# Show the sign-in provider's profile picture on the account control. The image
# is fetched by this server and relayed from this origin — the browser never
# reaches the provider's CDN, which is what keeps the Privacy Policy's "no
# third-party requests" true and keeps `img-src` at `'self' data:`.
#
# ABSENT IS FALSE, AND FALSE FAILS SILENTLY. Leaving this line out does not
# break sign-in: /auth/start simply asks for `openid email`, no `picture` claim
# ever arrives, and the glyph renders instead of a face. Nothing is logged,
# because nothing failed. That is exactly how it was missed on the first
# deployment — the setting is in `.env.example` and in SIGNIN-SETUP.md's table,
# and was in neither this file nor the `.env.prod` written from it. Check it by
# asking the running deployment rather than by reading a file:
#
#   curl -sD - -o /dev/null https://<origin>/auth/start | grep -o 'scope=[^&]*'
#
# `scope=openid+email+profile` is on; `scope=openid+email` is off.
#
# It needs three things on the Cognito side, not one, and they fail in three
# different ways — SIGNIN-SETUP.md, "Profile pictures need three things".
# Briefly: the Google IdP's authorized scopes must include `profile`; the app
# client must have **Profile** ticked, or asking for the scope fails the whole
# sign-in with `invalid_scope`; and the IdP must map `Picture → picture`, or
# everything succeeds and the claim is simply not in the token.
AUTH_AVATARS=true

# Required: compose refuses to start without it. The console role's password
# is set with ALTER ROLE (docs/operations/CONSOLE-SETUP.md), never the default.
CONSOLE_DATABASE_URL=postgres://karobakbak_console:CHANGE_ME@postgres:5432/karobakbak
